      "writtenOn": "2019-11-29T10:58:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Updated to remove usage of this word. This is a very useful word in programming though and often difficult to spell out. \"Idempotent\" means that a function has the same effect no matter how many times \u003e\u003d 1 it\u0027s called in a given environment. For example, println() is a textbook example of something that is not idempotent (if you call it twice, it prints twice).\n\nLogical AND and logical OR with a given operand are idempotent, but logical XOR is not. In code :\n\n  x \u0026 CONSTANT \u003d\u003d (x \u0026 CONSTANT) \u0026 CONSTANT\n  x | CONSTANT \u003d\u003d (x | CONSTANT) | CONSTANT\n  x ^ CONSTANT !\u003d (x ^ CONSTANT) ^ CONSTANT\n\nThe general formulation is :\n\n  if ∀ x, func(x) \u003d\u003d func(func(x)) then func is idempotent",
      "parentUuid": "167344fe_7e734f38",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6511,
        "startChar": 44,
        "endLine": 6511,
        "endChar": 54
